From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ysfaculty <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 23:05:34 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 181/182] hjviewer: review fixes for Windows device names in a
 download
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

On a Windows controller the download checks (libs/base fs/download.rs
validate_component, Dart DownloadPaths.validName) refused separators,
':' streams, <>"|?* and trailing dots or spaces, but not Windows device
names. A modified PC could list or send "COM1" or "nul.txt"; the
controller then opened '<folder>\COM1', '.download' and '.digest',
which Windows 10 opens as devices: a confusing overwrite question and a
failed download at best, bytes sent to a local device at worst.
Upstream has the same gap; 1.9.2 had it too.

Both checks now also refuse, on Windows only, a name whose part before
the first '.' (trailing spaces dropped) is CON, PRN, AUX, NUL, CONIN$,
CONOUT$, COM0-9 or LPT0-9 (also with a superscript 1-3), in any case and
with any extension. The job then ends as "오류 · 저장할 수 없는 파일 이름"
like the other refused names (the core's "invalid Windows download file
name" text, already mapped). Tests: the name rule on every platform
(download_tests.rs, test/download_paths_test.dart, a pure Dart test
that needs no bridge), and on Windows validated_target and
validate_download_name refusing such names.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
